$\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$
## ABSTRACT
Efficient separation and transfer of photogenerated electron/hole as well as enhanced visible light absorption play essential roles in photocatalytic reactions. To promote the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}.$ , a toxic heavy metal ion, multiwalled carbon nanotube (MWCNT) was introduced as an electron acceptor into $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ . This led to the growth of a willow leaf-like metal-organic framework (MOF) on an MWCNT backbone forming MWCNT $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-68(In) (PL-1), which showed a highly efficient transfer of photogenerated carriers. Moreover, MWCNT incorporation introduced more mesopores for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ diffusion and enhanced the visible light adsorption without lowering the conduction band position. As a result, the photocatalytic kinetic constant of PL-1 was found to be almost three times higher than that of the parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In). Thus, growing MOFs on MWCNTs provides a facile and promising solution for effective remediation of environmental pollution by utilizing solar energy. This work provides the first example of using MWCNT/MOF composites for photocatalytic reactions.
## 1 Introduction
$\mathrm{Cr(VI)},$ , which is a typical heavy metal ion contaminant usually discharged from leather tanning, paint-making, or steel manufacturing industrial processes, etc. [1, 2], has been identified as carcinogenic and its acute toxicity has been proved to cause mutagenic hazards to most of the organisms [3, 4]. Owing to its high solubility in water, which makes it mobile, various governments have put severe limitations on the amount of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ that can be present in drinking water as one of the priority controlled pollutants [5].
Various techniques such as membrane separation, adsorption, chemical precipitation, and photocatalysis have been used for removing $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from wastewater [6–8]. Currently, photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ by semiconductor photocatalysis is regarded as one of the most efficient strategies to remove $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from wastewater, because of the environment friendly nature of $\mathrm{Cr(III)}$ [5]. Moreover, $\mathrm{Cr(III)}$ can easily form precipitates or can be adsorbed on many materials in alkaline or even neutral solutions. Thus, it is generally immobile [9]. A number of studies have reported the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by various semiconductors such as CdS, $S\mathrm{n}S_{2},$ $\mathrm{Ag}_{2}S,$ ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ and $\mathsf{W O}_{3}$ [10–14]. However, the photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ reduction reported in these studies was rather limited with a low reaction rate. Moreover, the separation rate of photogenerated charges (electronhole pairs) is usually limited [15] and sulfide materials as photocatalysts always suffer from photocorrosion and may result in creating secondary pollutants because of their high toxicity [16]. Therefore, exploring and developing innovative photoactive catalysts with high stability and excellent photocatalytic performance for the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ is highly imperative.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a type of newly developed porous hybrid materials consisting of metal–oxo clusters and organic linkers and find potential applications in gas separation and storage, sensors, drug delivery and catalysis, etc. [17–20]. Upon irradiation, MOFs behave as light-sensitive semiconductors. They have shown great potential as heterogeneous photocatalysts for environmental remediation [21]. Since the development of MOF-5 as a photocatalyst, the development of photocatalytic MOFs has attracted great attention [22–27]. MOFs show excellent photocatalytic activity because of the coordination of transition metal centers and organic linkers, which results in various ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) transitions [28]. Currently, aminefunctionalized MOFs such as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) [29] and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) [30] have been reported to have the potential of photoreducing $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . However, the photoactivity of such catalysts is restricted because of fast charge recombinations occurring during the reduction. Incorporating such MOF semiconductors with electron acceptors is expected to overcome this challenge and to enhance their photocatalytic performance. However, very little literature is available on this strategy.
Owing to their excellent optical and electron transfer properties carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have been widely studied to improve the photoactivity of semiconductors [31]. The multiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs)/ CdS core–shell nanowires (with CNTs incorporated as electron acceptors) reported by Cao et al. [32] exhibited much higher photovoltage than CdS nanoparticles. The improvement in the photocatalytic activity of these nanowires after the incorporation of CNTs can be explained as follows: (a) CNTs act as electron acceptors, and thus could cause an efficient charge transfer and retard the charge recombination [33, 34]; (b) they act as photosensitizers, and thus could expand the visible light absorption leading to an enhanced light utilization efficiency [35, 36]; (c) the introduction of MWCNTs is believed to restrain the photocorrosion of semiconductors like CdS [37]. However, till date, there has been no report on the investigation of the photocatalytic properties of MWCNT/MOFs, despite the reports of enhanced adsorption ability shown by MWCNT/MIL-100(Fe) [38] and MWCNT/MIL-101 [39] for water and phenol, respectively.
In this context, we introduced MWCNTs into $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68(In) by a facile dynamic solvothermal method to enhance the photogenerated charge separation in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In). A series of MWCNT $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-68(In) composites were synthesized solvothermally and their structure, morphology, and optical and electronic properties were investigated by X-Ray Diffraction (XRD), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR), scanning electron microscope (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), photoluminescence (PL) spectra, and ultraviolet visible (UV-Vis) diffused reflectance spectrum (DRS) spectroscopy. The photocatalytic activity of MWCNT $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-68(In) for the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was evaluated and compared with that of the parent substrates. The photocatalytic mechanism for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ removal induced by UV/visible-light responsive MWCNT $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-68(In) was proposed. This work provides the first example of using a willow leaf-like MOF grown on a CNT backbone for light-driven photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
MWCNT $(95\%,$ , diameter, ${<}15\mathrm{nm}$ ; length, $\sim50\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ , Indium nitrate hydrate $\mathrm{(In(NO_{3})_{3}{\cdot}x H_{2}O,99.99\%),}$ , and 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ATA}$ , $99\%$ ) were supplied by Aladdin. Nitric acid $\left(\mathrm{HNO_{3}},70\%\ v/v\right)$ , sulfuric acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4},70\%v/v)$ , potassium dichromate $(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7},99\%)$ , 1,5-diphenylcarbazide $(99\%)$ , and citric acid monohydrate $(99.5\%)$ were obtained from Alfa Aesar. $^{\mathrm{N,N}}$ -dimethyl-formamide (DMF), ethanol (EtOH), and methanol $(\mathrm{MeOH})$ were purchased from Guanghua Chemicals. All reagents and solvents were of analytical grade.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MWCNT $/\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) composites
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-68(In)
$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) was synthesized by a dynamic solvothermal process, which is a modification of a previously reported solvothermal method [30]. First, $1.156\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.234\mathrm{~g~H}_{2}\mathrm{ATA}$ were dissolved into $12.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF in a flask under ultrasonication for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The clear suspension so obtained was stirred constantly and was refluxed at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ in an oil bath. After cooling to room temperature, the obtained light yellow mixture was collected and washed several times with DMF and was immersed in anhydrous methanol for three days to remove the guest molecules present in the pores. Finally, the product was centrifuged and dried at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of MWCNT/NH2-MIL-68(In)
Prior to the synthesis of the MWCNT $/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) composite, the raw MWCNTs were purified and surface functionalized, as per a reported procedure [40]. MWCNTs $(1.0\mathrm{g})$ were oxidized in $70\%$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ $(70~\mathrm{mL})$ ) in a flask with reflux at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{h}$ . After natural cooling, the mixture was diluted and washed repeatedly with deionized water until the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of the filtrate reached ${\sim}5.5$ . The filtered powder was then dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in vacuum for $12\mathrm{h}$ . The MWCNTs were then acid treated after which most of the impurities were removed and several types of functional groups including hydroxyl, carbonyl, and carboxyl groups were introduced on their surface [41, 42].
## 2.3 Characterization
Powder XRD (PXRD) analyses were carried out on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with a monochromatized $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ radiation ${\mathit{\Omega}}^{\prime}\lambda=0.15418\mathrm{nm}$ ) source at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40\mathrm{mA}$ . The infrared absorption spectra were analyzed with a Nicolet 6700 FTIR spectrometric analyzer using KBr pellets. The morphology of the synthesized samples was examined using SEM and TEM. The chemical composition of the samples was analyzed by XPS (ESCALAB 250Xi). UV-Vis DRS were recorded on a UV-Vis spectrophotometer (UV-3600, Shimadzu, Japan) with $\mathrm{BaSO_{4}}$ as a reflectance standard. An ASAP 2460 V2.01 apparatus (Micromeritics Instrument Corp., USA) was used to examine the
Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and porous structure using the nitrogen adsorption and desorption isotherms of the samples measured at $77\mathrm{K}$ after being degassed in vacuum at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The PL spectra were observed on a fluorescence spectrometer (Hitachi F-4600) with an excitation wavelength of $320\mathrm{nm}$ . The electrochemical experiments were conducted on an electrochemical workstation (CHI660D Instruments) using a three-electrode system.
## 2.4 Photocatalytic reduction of $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{Cr}(V I)}$
The evaluation of the photocatalytic activity of the photocatalysts for the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was conducted under an irradiation from a 500 W Xe lamp. The distance between the light source and the reactor containing the reaction mixture was fixed at $15\mathrm{cm}$ and the light intensity was about 25 and $30\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ under visible irradiation and UV-Vis irradiation, respectively. The reaction system was prepared by dispersing $40\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared photocatalyst into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of a ${\sf K}_{2}{\sf C}{\bf r}_{2}{\sf O}_{7}$ solution having a $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration of $10\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ in a $50\mathrm{mL}$ cylindrical Pyrex reactor. Subsequently, a certain amount of citric acid was added into the suspension to adjust the solution $\mathsf{p H}$ to ${\sim}4.0$ . After being stirred in dark for $60\mathrm{min}$ to achieve the adsorption/desorption equilibrium, the suspensions were irradiated in open air at room temperature with the addition of methanol $(200~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ . At given time intervals, about $2\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension was withdrawn and centrifuged to separate the photocatalysts for analysis. All the photocatalytic measurements mentioned here were repeated twice to ensure the reliability of the results. After the photocatalytic reaction, the catalysts were recycled for cycle tests.
## 2.5 Analytical methods
The photocatalytic performance (under visible irradiation) of the photocatalysts was evaluated with a $420\mathrm{nm}$ UV cut-off filter. The concentration of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was analyzed using the diphenylcarbazide (DPC) colorimetric method with a detection limit of $0.005\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ [17, 18, 43]. Typically, $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ of the supernatant was mixed with $9.0\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.2\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ in a $10.0\mathrm{mL}$ colorimetric tube. Subsequently,
$0.2\mathrm{mL}$ of freshly prepared $0.25\%$ $(w/v)$ DPC in acetone was added into the colorimetric tube. The mixture was kept for $15\mathrm{min}$ to ensure full color development after being shaken for about $30\mathrm{{s}}$ . The absorbance of the colored $\mathrm{Cr(VI){\-}D P C}$ complex solution was measured at ${k_{\operatorname*{max}}=540\mathrm{nm}}$ using a UV-visible spectrophotometer with the reagent blank solution (deionized (DI) water) as the reference. The observed absorbance intensities at different irradiation times could be converted to the reduction ratio of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ with the following equation
where $C_{0}$ and $C_{t}$ are the absorbance intensities of the photocatalysts when illuminated for 0 and $t\ \mathrm{min},$ respectively.
## 2.6 Photoelectrochemistry measurements
An electrochemical workstation (CHI660D Instruments) was employed for the electrochemical experiments. For the preparation of the electrodes [44], $5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{MOF}$ photocatalysts were dispersed in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and the resulting mixtures were ultrasonicated for $1\mathrm{h}.0.3\mathrm{mL}$ of the colloidal solution so obtained was dropped onto a pretreated indium-tin oxide (ITO) surface and was allowed to dry in air for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ at room temperature followed by drying at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ . An electrochemical analyzer was employed to record the photocurrents in a standard three-electrode system, which was composed of a working electrodes (PL- $x)$ , counter electrode (a Pt foil), and reference electrode (a saturated calomel electrode, SCE). $0.1\mathrm{{M}}$ ${\tt N a}_{2}S{\tt O}_{4}$ aqueous solution was used as the electrolyte.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization
The PXRD patterns of MWCNT, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In), and the MWCNT $/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ -MIL-68(In) composites are shown in Fig. 1. The diffraction patterns of the composites were in good agreement with those reported previously for [30, 45] and were identical to the simulated PXRD patterns, indicating that the crystalline nature of the parental $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) remained intact in the composite. Nevertheless, the typical diffraction peak of MWCNT was absent in the composite. This can be attributed to the low MWCNT content or to the high dispersion of MWCNT [46].
The FT-IR spectra of MWCNT, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}68(\mathrm{In}).$ , and PL-1 are shown in Fig. 2. The spectrum for MWCNT shows wide band centered at $3,450\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the $\mathrm{O-H}$ groups (resulting from strongly H-bonded– COOH ) present on their surface [47]. The weak peaks observed in the $2,880{-}2,900\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ range indicate the presence of $-\mathrm{CH}_{n}$ species [48, 49]. The band at $1{,}683\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the stretching of the CNT backbone [50]. Nevertheless, no obvious adsorption bands of
MWCNT were observed in PL-1. Meanwhile, the main adsorption bands observed for $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) were also present in the spectrum for MWCNT/ $\mathrm{NH_{2}\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In}),$ indicating that the incorporation of MWCNT did not disturb the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ -MIL-68(In) crystal structure.
Figure 3 shows the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of MWCNT, pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In), and PL-1. Both $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) and PL-1 showed the same type I isotherms (based on the IUPAC classification), suggesting that micropores were predominant in the photocatalysts. Interestingly, small indistinct hysteresis loops were also observed, indicating the presence of a small number of mesopores in PL-1 for the capillary condensation [51], while MWCNTs exhibited a larger hysteresis loop, indicating the presence of a large number of mesopores. The BET surface area and pore volume of the samples are listed in Table 1. MWCNTs had a BET surface area of $152.3\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , which is consistent with that reported previously [52, 53]. Unlike some previous reports [39, 54], in this study, the BET surface area of PL-1 increased slightly compared to that of the pristine $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}68(\mathrm{In}).$ . Similar trend has been observed for ZIF-8/CNT [55], and CNT@MIL-68(Al) [56]. In the both cases, a low CNT loading improved the BET surface area of the MOF, though a further increase in the CNT loading decreased the BET surface area. In our case, MWCNTs were believed to provide numerous nucleation sites for the growth of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) crystals and to increase the nucleation rate of the MOF crystals over their sidewalls, leading to an increase in the BET surface area. This increase was expected to surpass the interference effect of MWCNTs on the intrinsic porosity of the composites [38, 55, 56]. The porosity results also suggest that MWCNTs were successfully integrated into $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ . The increased porosity of the composites was believed to promote the diffusion of ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ , and thus to improve their photocatalytic efficiency.
The texture of MWCNT $/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) could be observed from the SEM and TEM images shown in Fig. 4. For comparison, the SEM images of the acidtreated MWCNTs and pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ are also included. It can be seen from Figs. 4(a) and 4(b) that the purified and acid-treated MWCNT showed a tube structure with a diameter of ${\sim}20\mathrm{nm}$ . Figures 4(c)
and 4(d) show that the composites exhibited a welldispersed, willow leaf-like structure, which may be attributed to the formation of building blocks of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) crystals during the synthesis. For the MWCNT $/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) composite, it can be observed that the willow leaf-like structure of In-MOF grew along with the tube structure of MWCNTs, and the MWCNTs were observed to be encapsulated by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68(In). Hence, only a few MWCNTs could be observed (Figs. 4(e) and 4(f)). In order to obtain a better insight into the morphology of the $\mathrm{MWCNT/NH_{2}}\mathrm{.}$ - MIL-68(In) composite, the TEM images of PL-1 were analyzed (Figs. $4(\mathrm{g)}$ and $4(\mathrm{h}))$ . The willow leaf-like structure of In-MOF could still be clearly seen, while the tubular structure of the incorporated MWCNTs was encapsulated and sheltered by In-MOF.
XPS was conducted to gain insight into the changes occurring in the chemical environment of PL-1, and the XPS patterns are shown in Fig. 5. After the introduction of MWCNTs, a new shoulder peak at $533.2\mathrm{eV}$ appeared in the O 1s core level spectra for PL-1, which corresponds to the residual aldehyde groups on the acid-treated MWCNTs (Fig. 5(a)) [50]. A negative shift of $0.4\mathrm{eV}$ was observed for PL-1 in the In 3d spectrum, as shown in Fig. 6(b), which might have resulted from an enhanced electron density because of the surrounded $\pi$ -rich conjugation structures of MWCNT [57]. The XPS results further confirmed the successful incorporation of MWCNTs in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIIL}{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ in the PL-1 composite. These results were consistent with those of the SEM/TEM analyses.
The optical property of the materials varied significantly because of the incorporation of MWCNTs, as shown in Fig. 6. The bare MWCNT exhibited strong light absorption in the $200{-}800\mathrm{nm}$ range. This is consistent with the results obtained in a previous report [58]. $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In})$ showed a weak visible light absorption with the edge at about $470\mathrm{nm}$ . After the introduction of MWCNTs, the PL- $x$ composites showed a red-shift, indicating their band-gap narrowing. The inset of Fig. 6 shows the intercepts of the tangents of $(A h v)^{1/2}$ vs. photon energy of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{PL}-x$ composites. The corresponding band-gap was estimated to be 2.63, 2.43, 2.36, and $2.30\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In), PL-1, PL-2, and PL-3, respectively. Moreover, with an increase in the amount of MWCNTs added to the PL- $x$ composites, the intensity of the visible light absorption became stronger, which is further confirmed by the color change from (a) to (d), as observed from the photographs of the four samples. An enhanced visible light absorption was expected to be beneficial to the improvement of the photocatalytic activity of the PL- $x$ composites.
## 3.2 $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{Cr}(V I)}$ photoreduction
Figure 7(a) shows the photoreduction efficiency of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ over the PL- $x$ $(x=1,2,3)$ ) composites and the parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ under visible light irradiation (Xenon lamp with UV filter, light intensity of $25\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ). It can be seen that PL-1 showed the highest photoreduction efficiency among the tested samples. However, the improvement was not significant. In comparison, $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ photoreduction was also evaluated under irradiation using a xenon lamp (without UV filter, light intensity of $30\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ). As shown in Fig. 7(b), PL-1 could reduce $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ completely within $60\mathrm{min}$ under UV-Vis irradiation. It should be noted that the improvement in the photoreduction efficiency of PL-1 was more pronounced under UV-Vis irradiation than that under visible light only, which can be attributed to the full utilization of the electron-transfer potential of MWCNTs when more photogenerated electrons were generated. A further increase in the amount of MWCNTs resulted in a decrease in the photocatalytic activity, which may be ascribed to the “covering effect” [59]: (1) excessive MWCNTs might cover the surface active sites of $\mathrm{NH_{2}\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In});$ (2) the overloaded MWCNTs might shield the light absorption by $\mathrm{NH_{2}\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In}),$ , and thus decrease the number of photo-induced electrons.
Control experiments were performed to confirm the photoreduction activity of the PL-1 composite. Figure 8(a) shows the variation of the concentration of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\ (C/C_{0})$ over time under various conditions. In the absence of the photocatalysts, upon irradiation with UV-Vis light for $120\mathrm{min},$ , no change was observed in the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration. Furthermore, the addition of MWCNTs resulted in a slight decrease in the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration. This is attributed to the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by MWCNTs. The $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration showed almost no decrease under the dark condition even in the presence of the photocatalysts, suggesting that the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ reduction was a photo-induced process utilizing the PL- $x$ composites as the photocatalysts. It should be noted that the PL-1 composite showed much higher $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ photoreduction efficiency than the pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In})$ as well as the physical mixture of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) and MWCNTs, indicating that the interaction between MWCNTs and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68(In) in PL-1 had a significant effect on the photoreduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
MWCNTs into $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) is an effective method to promote the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
The reusability and stability (water stability and photo stability) of MOFs and MOF-based materials are essential for real-time applications [60]. Recycling experiments were carried out to examine the stability of PL-1. PL-1 was regenerated through simple filtration and was washed with DI water and ethanol for several times followed by drying at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As shown in Fig. 9, almost no loss of photocatalytic activity was observed after four cycles. The stability of PL-1 was also confirmed by the XRD and FTIR analysis (Fig. 10) results. These results well illustrate the high reusability and stability of the MWCNT $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-68(In) composite, thus making it a potential candidate for practical applications.
## 3.3 Illustration of photocatalytic mechanism
In(III) MOFs and In(III) MOF-based materials have demonstrated semiconductor properties for photocatalytic reactions such as the photodegradation of amoxicillin [61], photoreduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ [30], etc. In this work, MWCNT was encapsulated in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68(In) for the first time to facilitate a photocatalytic reaction. The introduction of MWCNTs made the following contributions to the photoreduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ : (1) it added new mesopores for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ diffusion, (2) enhanced visible light absorption, and (3) limited the recombination of photo-induced electrons/holes.
As mentioned earlier, the incorporation of MWCNTs in PL-1 increased the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) pore volume (Table 1), indicating the formation of new pore structures. To further understand the formed new pores, the pore size distributions of PL-1 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In})$ were measured, as shown in Fig. 11. Compared to the parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In}).$ , the PL composites showed an enhanced porosity in the mesopore portion $(2{-}20\mathrm{nm})$ , which was expected to be beneficial for the diffusion of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)},$ , and thus could improve the photoreduction efficiency of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
Enhancing the visible light absorption of MOFs is one of the most common strategies to improve their photocatalytic performance [60]. In this work, as analyzed with the UV-Vis diffusion, the incorporation of MWCNTs in the PL composites enhanced their visible light absorption, as evidenced by their narrowed band gaps ( $\mathrm{from}\ 2.63$ to $2.43\mathrm{eV}\cdot$ ). Valence band XPS analysis was carried out to monitor the changes in the band positions. From Fig. 12, the valence band positions of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In})$ and PL-1 were estimated to be 2.76 and $2.54\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Compared to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In}).$ , PL-1 showed a ca. $0.22\mathrm{eV}$ upward shift in the valence band top. This result is in agreement with the narrowed band gap of LP-1 $(0.20\mathrm{eV})$ upon the incorporation of MWCNTs. Therefore, the conduction band position of PL-1 remained almost the same as that of the parent NH2- MIL-68(In), which is crucial to maintain the high reduction potential and photoreduction capability of PL-1 as the parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}68(\mathrm{In})$ .
The recombination of photoexcited electron-hole pairs directly affects the probability of surface redox reactions [33]. Steady-state PL was employed to gain an insight into the transfer of photo-generated charge carriers in PL-1. As shown in Fig. 13(a), the emissions of PL-1 were remarkably quenched as compared to the parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ -MIL-68(In), confirming that the charge carriers generated by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) were favorably transferred to the electron acceptor (MWCNTs). As a result, the charge recombination was retarded [62]. This result was further supported by the higher photocurrents of PL-1 than that of the parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68(In), as shown in Fig. 13(b). It can be concluded that the charge transfer was facilitated by the incorporation of MWCNTs in PL-1, resulting in a decreased recombination of photo-induced $\mathrm{e^{-}/h^{+},}$ , leading to an improved $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ photoreduction efficiency.
To summarize, the photoreduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ using PL-1 is illustrated in Fig. 14. Under light irradiation, the electrons of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ moved from the valence band to the conduction band. The MWCNTs with their 1D hierarchical structure acted as electron acceptors to ensure the transfer of the photogenerated electrons from the conduction band of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In). Moreover, the newly generated mesopores in PL-1 facilitated the diffusion of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to the photocatalyst surface to meet the migrated electrons, making the photoreduction take place.
## 4 Conclusion
In this work, growing willow leaf-like $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL$68(\mathrm{{In})}$ on an MWCNT backbone was demonstrated to be an effective strategy for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ photoreduction. The composite was synthesized by a facile dynamic hydrothermal method. In view of the intrinsic properties of MWCNTs, the as-prepared PL-1 composite exhibited a much higher $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ photoreduction efficiency than the pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In). The mechanism behind the enhanced $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ photoreduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) upon the incorporation of MWCNTs was elaborated. First, the incorporated MWCNTs generated new mesopores to facilitate the diffusion of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . Second, the incorporated MWCNTs served as an excellent photosensitizer to enhance the visible light absorption without lowering the conduction band position. Lastly, the incorporated MWCNTs not only accepted the photogenerated electrons from $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(In) but also provided a platform for them to migrate, ensuring an efficient charge transfer and a reduced $\mathrm{e^{-}/h^{+}}$ recombination. The work provides the first example of using MWCNT/MOF composites for photocatalytic reduction and demonstrates the potential of MWCNT/MOF hybrids for photocatalysis.
